// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Charging — the seam between what happened and what is billed (PI-8)
//
// ⚠️ THIS PROGRAMME IMPLEMENTS NO INVOICING, AND NOTHING IN THIS FILE HOLDS A
//   TAX FIGURE. rcln already has an invoice engine, a tax engine and a document
//   engine, all working end to end for appointment invoicing. Pharmacy and
//   inventory HAND WORK TO THEM. A charge request is the structured hand-off:
//   what was supplied, to whom, at which counter, at what moment, under which
//   charge policy, at which price and in which tax category. Everything after
//   that — apportionment, tax lines, totals, numbering, the PDF — is
//   `services/invoicing` and `@rcln/tax`, unchanged.
//
//   `charge_requests.invoice_id` is the only link back, and it is written BY
//   invoicing. Nothing in this programme inserts an `invoice_item`. (The design
//   document says `invoice_item_id`; see the column for why an item id cannot be
//   the link.)
//
// ── WHY A CHARGE REQUEST IS A ROW AND NOT A FUNCTION CALL ────────────────────
// The obvious shape is for `createDispense` to call `createInvoiceFor…` at the
// end of its transaction. It is wrong in three ways, and PI-ADR-005 is the ADR:
//
//   1. NOT EVERYTHING SUPPLIED IS BILLED. A consumed glove produces no invoice
//      line; an implant does. That decision is the CHARGE POLICY, it is a
//      clinic's commercial position, and it has to be recorded as having been
//      made — including when it decided not to bill.
//   2. SOME OF IT IS A HUMAN'S DECISION. `OPTIONAL` means somebody at the desk
//      decides, and a decision nobody can see before it reaches a patient's bill
//      is not a decision anybody made.
//   3. THE BILL IS NOT ALWAYS RAISED AT THE COUNTER. Three supplies across a
//      morning are frequently one invoice, and an inpatient's are one at
//      discharge. A per-supply invoice call cannot express that; a queue of
//      requests can.
//
// ── PHI ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// A charge request names a patient and a medicine — the same fact
// `dispense_lines` carries, and treated the same way. `description` is
// PHI-capable free text (it becomes an invoice line a patient reads). Ids only
// in logs and in Redis; nothing here in a URL.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

/// Should this reach a bill at all?
///
/// ⚠️ SEPARATE FROM CONSUMPTION, WHICH IS THE WHOLE POINT (PI-ADR-005). "It left
///   the shelf" and "somebody owes for it" are two facts, and a system that
///   derives the second from the first bills for gauze.
enum ChargePolicy {
  /// Consumed, never charged — gloves, disinfectant, the swab.
  NEVER_BILL
  /// Consumed, and its cost is already inside a procedure or consultation fee —
  /// the anaesthetic, the suture on a sutured wound. ⚠️ NOT the same as
  /// `NEVER_BILL`: the clinic IS recovering the cost, through another line, and
  /// a costing report must be able to tell the two apart.
  INCLUDED_IN_SERVICE
  /// Its own invoice line — a dispensed medicine, an implant.
  SEPARATELY_BILLABLE
  /// A human decides, at the charge-review step. The request sits `PENDING`
  /// until they do, and no invoice may be raised from it before then.
  OPTIONAL
  /// The payer contract decides.
  ///
  /// ⚠️ THERE IS NO CONTRACT ENGINE, AND THIS RESOLVES TO A HUMAN DECISION
  ///   EXACTLY AS `OPTIONAL` DOES — see the note on `ChargeRequestStatus`. It is
  ///   a distinct member rather than an alias because the REASON the desk is
  ///   being asked is different, and the day a contract engine lands it is this
  ///   member's call sites that change and not `OPTIONAL`'s. Recorded as
  ///   accepted debt in KNOWN_ISSUES.
  CONTRACT_DEFINED
  /// The regulatory or tax configuration decides. Same treatment and the same
  /// reason as `CONTRACT_DEFINED`.
  JURISDICTION_CONFIGURED
}

/// Which tier of the precedence chain produced the policy on a charge request.
///
/// ⚠️ SNAPSHOTTED ON THE REQUEST BESIDE THE POLICY ITSELF, so an old bill can
///   still explain itself after the rules have been edited. "Why was this
///   billed?" is answered by the row, never by re-resolving — the resolution
///   would run against today's table and today's table is not what decided.
enum ChargePolicyScope {
  /// A rule naming this exact product.
  PRODUCT
  /// A rule naming a category this product is in.
  PRODUCT_CATEGORY
  /// A rule naming this product's type.
  PRODUCT_TYPE
  /// No rule matched. `SEPARATELY_BILLABLE` for a medicine or a vaccine,
  /// `NEVER_BILL` for everything else — see `defaultChargePolicy`.
  DEFAULT
}

/// Where a charge request is.
///
/// ```text
///   PENDING ──▶ INVOICED
///      │
///      ├──▶ SUPPRESSED    (policy said no, or a human said no)
///      └──▶ CANCELLED     (the supply it cites was reversed before billing)
/// ```
///
/// ⚠️ `SUPPRESSED` IS A TERMINAL SUCCESS, NOT A FAILURE. A glove that produced no
///   invoice line reached exactly the state it should, and the row exists so the
///   clinic can prove the decision was taken rather than missed.
enum ChargeRequestStatus {
  /// Waiting to be billed, or waiting for a human where the policy asked for
  /// one. ⚠️ The only state from which an invoice may be raised.
  PENDING
  /// Deliberately not billed. Carries the reason.
  SUPPRESSED
  /// On an invoice. `invoice_id` is set, and it is written by
  /// `services/invoicing`.
  INVOICED
  /// The supply behind it was undone before it reached a bill.
  CANCELLED
}

/// What kind of movement this request is the money side of.
///
/// ⚠️ A REVERSAL IS NOT A NEGATIVE SUPPLY. Its quantity is POSITIVE and its
///   direction is in this column, for the reason `stock_ledger` keeps the sign
///   out of the quantity: a negative number in a money column is one missing
///   minus sign away from a credit that charges.
enum ChargeRequestKind {
  /// Something was supplied. The ordinary case.
  SUPPLY
  /// Something came back. Reduces what is owed — by cancelling the original
  /// request where it was never billed, and by a CREDIT NOTE where it was.
  REVERSAL
}

/// A clinic's standing answer to "is this billed?".
///
/// ⚠️ NOT EFFECTIVE-DATED, DELIBERATELY, AND THE SNAPSHOT IS WHY. A charge
///   request records the policy AND the tier that produced it at the moment of
///   supply, so history is answerable from the request rather than from this
///   table's past. Effective dating here would be a second history of the same
///   fact, free to disagree with the first.
///
/// ⚠️ THE PRECEDENCE CHAIN IS SHORTER THAN BILLING_INTEGRATION.md's. That
///   document lists eight tiers, five of which name a `procedure` or a `payer`.
///   Neither entity exists: procedures are PI-9, blocked on `encounters`, and
///   there is no payer-contract model anywhere in this repository. The three
///   expressible tiers are built; the rest arrive with the entities they name,
///   and `ChargePolicyScope` is where the new members go. Guessing at the shape
///   of a rule against a table that does not exist is the mistake PI-5 records
///   about `regulatory_decisions`.
model ChargePolicyRule {
  id             String @id @default(uuid()) @db.Uuid
  organizationId String @map("organization_id") @db.Uuid

  /// ⚠️ EXACTLY ONE OF THE THREE IS SET, BY CHECK. A rule that named both a
  ///   product and a type would have two places in the precedence chain and the
  ///   resolver would have to pick one, which is a coin toss dressed as a rule.
  ///
  /// Plain FKs into platform-extensible tables, so both get a RESTRICTIVE
  /// `*_visible` policy — without them a clinic attaches another clinic's
  /// private product to its own rule and reads the name back through the join
  /// on the charge-policy screen (KI-3).
  productId         String?      @map("product_id") @db.Uuid
  productCategoryId String?      @map("product_category_id") @db.Uuid
  productType       ProductType? @map("product_type")

  policy ChargePolicy

  /// Why the clinic decided this. Not PHI — it is about a catalogue row.
  note String? @db.Text

  createdAt DateTime  @default(now()) @map("created_at") @db.Timestamptz(6)
  updatedAt DateTime  @updatedAt @map("updated_at") @db.Timestamptz(6)
  updatedBy String?   @map("updated_by") @db.Uuid
  deletedAt DateTime? @map("deleted_at") @db.Timestamptz(6)

  organization    Organization     @relation(fields: [organizationId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  /// Restrict on both: a rule that has decided a bill outlives the screen that
  /// made it. A product is deactivated, never deleted.
  product         Product?         @relation("ChargePolicyRuleProduct", fields: [productId], references: [id], onDelete: Restrict)
  productCategory ProductCategory? @relation("ChargePolicyRuleCategory", fields: [productCategoryId], references: [id], onDelete: Restrict)
  updater         User?            @relation("ChargePolicyRuleUpdatedBy", fields: [updatedBy], references: [id], onDelete: SetNull)
  /// The charge requests this rule decided. `Restrict` on the other side — see
  /// `ChargeRequest.policyRuleId`.
  chargeRequests  ChargeRequest[]

  /// ⚠️ NULLS NOT DISTINCT in the migration. Every scoping column is nullable,
  ///   and a plain unique does not constrain NULLs — without it a clinic holds
  ///   two different answers for one product and the planner decides which one a
  ///   patient is charged under.
  @@unique([organizationId, productId, productCategoryId, productType])
  @@unique([organizationId, id])
  /// The resolver's read: every candidate rule for one product, in one index.
  @@index([organizationId, productId])
  @@index([organizationId, productCategoryId])
  @@index([organizationId, productType])
  @@map("charge_policy_rules")
}

/// What a clinic SELLS a product for.
///
/// ⚠️ A SELLING PRICE IS NOT AN INVENTORY COST, AND NEITHER IS DERIVED FROM THE
///   OTHER (PI-ADR-010). `product_cost_averages` is what the clinic paid;
///   this is what it charges. A margin percentage that turned one into the other
///   would make every purchase price change silently restate the shelf price,
///   which is a commercial decision no procurement clerk should be able to take
///   by keying in a delivery.
///
/// ⚠️ THE SAME SHAPE AS `fee_schedule_entries`, DELIBERATELY, AND GATED BY THE
///   SAME PERMISSION. `billing.fee_schedule.manage` already means "may set what
///   this clinic charges", which is exactly this. A second pricing precedence
///   rule with its own permission code is how a screen quotes one number and the
///   bill states another.
///
/// Precedence: branch override beats the organization default. The payer-contract
/// and manual-override tiers BILLING_INTEGRATION.md names are, respectively, an
/// engine that does not exist and the cashier editing the draft — which the
/// invoice engine already supports and this table therefore does not.
model ProductPrice {
  id             String  @id @default(uuid()) @db.Uuid
  organizationId String  @map("organization_id") @db.Uuid
  /// NULL = the organization's default, which every branch inherits.
  branchId       String? @map("branch_id") @db.Uuid

  /// Plain FK into a platform-extensible table — see `product_visible`.
  productId String @map("product_id") @db.Uuid

  /// ⚠️ THE UNIT THE PRICE IS PER, AND IT IS NOT NECESSARILY THE BASE UNIT. A
  ///   clinic prices a strip, not a tablet, and an invoice line reading
  ///   "2 × ₹45.00" has to mean two of whatever the patient was handed. The
  ///   charge request converts the base quantity into THIS unit through the one
  ///   conversion graph the ledger uses, and stores the result as the invoice
  ///   line's quantity.
  unitId String @map("unit_id") @db.Uuid

  /// ⚠️ Decimal, never a float, and never minor units in an integer column here:
  ///   this is a stored money value and matches `invoice_items.unit_price`
  ///   exactly, which is what lets the two be compared without a conversion.
  amount   Decimal @db.Decimal(14, 2)
  /// ISO 4217. Denormalised from the organization so a price row can be read
  /// without a second query, and CHECKed against nothing — a clinic that changes
  /// its currency has a bigger problem than this column.
  currency String  @default("INR") @db.Char(3)

  createdAt DateTime  @default(now()) @map("created_at") @db.Timestamptz(6)
  updatedAt DateTime  @updatedAt @map("updated_at") @db.Timestamptz(6)
  updatedBy String?   @map("updated_by") @db.Uuid
  deletedAt DateTime? @map("deleted_at") @db.Timestamptz(6)

  organization Organization @relation(fields: [organizationId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  branch       Branch?      @relation(fields: [organizationId, branchId], references: [organizationId, id], onDelete: Cascade)
  product      Product      @relation("ProductPriceProduct", fields: [productId], references: [id], onDelete: Restrict)
  unit         UnitOfMeasure @relation("ProductPriceUnit", fields: [unitId], references: [id], onDelete: Restrict)
  updater      User?        @relation("ProductPriceUpdatedBy", fields: [updatedBy], references: [id], onDelete: SetNull)

  /// ⚠️ NULLS NOT DISTINCT in the migration, for the reason
  ///   `fee_schedule_entries` needs it: `branch_id` is nullable and one clinic
  ///   may hold exactly one organization-wide price per product per currency.
  @@unique([organizationId, productId, branchId, currency])
  @@unique([organizationId, id])
  /// The resolver's read: both candidate rows for one product, in one index.
  @@index([organizationId, productId])
  @@map("product_prices")
}

/// One thing that happened, offered to billing.
///
/// ⚠️ IT IS WRITTEN IN THE SAME TRANSACTION AS THE SUPPLY THAT CAUSED IT. A
///   charge request that can commit independently of its dispense is one that
///   will, on the day the dispense rolls back — and the clinic bills for
///   medicine that never left the shelf. Same discipline as the ledger legs and
///   the regulatory snapshot, and for the same reason.
///
/// ⚠️ AND ITS FAILURE MUST NEVER STOP THE SUPPLY. That is a tension with the
///   line above and it is resolved in one direction: raising the request is
///   arithmetic over rows already read, so it does not fail for reasons a
///   pharmacist could hit. Where a price or a tax category is MISSING the
///   request is still written, `unit_price` or `tax_category` is NULL, and the
///   gap is visible on the charge-review screen. A missing price is a
///   configuration gap, never a reason to refuse to hand somebody their
///   medicine.
model ChargeRequest {
  id             String @id @default(uuid()) @db.Uuid
  organizationId String @map("organization_id") @db.Uuid
  /// The counter it happened at. The place of supply, the number series and the
  /// RLS boundary — the same three things it is on an invoice.
  branchId       String @map("branch_id") @db.Uuid

  /// Which invoice source this becomes. ⚠️ CHECKed to `PHARMACY` or `INVENTORY`
  ///   — those are the two this programme hands over, and an `APPOINTMENT`
  ///   charge request would be a second door into a path
  ///   `appointment-billing.service.ts` already owns.
  sourceType InvoiceSourceType   @map("source_type")
  kind       ChargeRequestKind   @default(SUPPLY)
  status     ChargeRequestStatus @default(PENDING)

  /// ⚠️ ONE TYPED REFERENCE PER SOURCE, NEVER A POLYMORPHIC `source_id`. Exactly
  ///   the argument `invoices` makes in its own header: a loose uuid cannot be
  ///   foreign-keyed, and the composite `(organization_id, id)` reference is what
  ///   makes a cross-tenant link unrepresentable (ADR-0004).
  ///
  ///   `dispense_line_id` is set on a pharmacy SUPPLY;
  ///   `dispense_return_line_id` on a pharmacy REVERSAL; `consumption_line_id`
  ///   on an `INVENTORY` request of either kind.
  ///
  /// ⚠️ `consumption_line_id` ARRIVED IN PI-9's OWN MIGRATION, WHICH IS WHAT
  ///   PI-8 SAID WOULD HAPPEN. It carries no `..._return_line_id` twin because a
  ///   consumption reversal is another `clinical_consumptions` ROW with its own
  ///   lines (`CONSUMPTION_REVERSAL`), not a separate return table — so one
  ///   column serves both directions and `kind` says which it is.
  dispenseLineId       String? @map("dispense_line_id") @db.Uuid
  dispenseReturnLineId String? @map("dispense_return_line_id") @db.Uuid
  consumptionLineId    String? @map("consumption_line_id") @db.Uuid

  /// What was supplied. Plain FKs into platform-extensible tables.
  productId String @map("product_id") @db.Uuid
  /// ⚠️ PHI, AND NULLABLE FOR THE REASON `dispenses.patient_id` IS. A counter
  ///   sale is frequently to somebody who is not a patient here.
  patientId String? @map("patient_id") @db.Uuid

  /// ⚠️ WHEN THE SUPPLY HAPPENED, WHICH IS NOT WHEN IT IS BILLED. It becomes the
  ///   invoice's `supplied_at`, so it selects the effective-dated tax rule and
  ///   the registration: a bill raised in April for a March supply is taxed as
  ///   March was. Copied from `dispenses.dispensed_at`, never `now()`.
  occurredAt DateTime @map("occurred_at") @db.Timestamptz(6)

  /// ⚠️ THE QUANTITY IN BASE UNITS AND THE QUANTITY ON THE BILL ARE DIFFERENT
  ///   NUMBERS AT DIFFERENT SCALES, AND BOTH ARE STORED.
  ///   `quantity_base` is `Decimal(18,6)` and reconciles against `stock_ledger`.
  ///   `quantity` is `Decimal(14,3)` in `unit_id` and is what
  ///   `invoice_items.quantity` will hold — a coarser, human-facing number ("2
  ///   strips") on a frozen column of an issued document. The conversion happens
  ///   here, once, and is deliberate; BILLING_INTEGRATION.md § Invoice
  ///   construction is the note.
  quantityBase Decimal @map("quantity_base") @db.Decimal(18, 6)
  quantity     Decimal @db.Decimal(14, 3)
  /// The unit `quantity` and `unit_price` are both expressed in.
  unitId       String  @map("unit_id") @db.Uuid

  /// ⚠️ EVERY FIELD FROM HERE TO `tax_category` IS A SNAPSHOT TAKEN AT SUPPLY.
  ///   The policy table and the price table are both live and both editable, and
  ///   an old bill that re-resolved itself would restate a document somebody has
  ///   already been handed. What was true at supply is what stays on the row —
  ///   the same rule every tax field on `invoices` follows.
  policy      ChargePolicy
  policyScope ChargePolicyScope @map("policy_scope")
  /// Which rule produced it, where one did. NULL on `DEFAULT`.
  ///
  /// ⚠️ `Restrict`, AND `SetNull` IS NOT AVAILABLE HERE — the same trap
  ///   `Membership.lastBranchId` documents. The FK is composite, so SetNull
  ///   would null EVERY referencing column including `organization_id`, which is
  ///   NOT NULL, and the delete would fail at runtime with a constraint
  ///   violation rather than doing what it says. `charge_policy_rules` is
  ///   soft-deleted for exactly this reason: a rule that has decided a bill is
  ///   retired with `deleted_at`, never removed.
  policyRuleId String? @map("policy_rule_id") @db.Uuid

  /// What the patient should see on the line. ⚠️ PHI-capable: it names a
  /// medicine, and it becomes `invoice_items.description` verbatim.
  description String @db.VarChar(255)

  /// ⚠️ NULL IS A VISIBLE CONFIGURATION GAP AND NEVER A DEFAULT — the same rule
  ///   `resolveTaxCategory` follows. A NULL price means the clinic has not
  ///   priced this product; a NULL category propagates to `UNRATED` and the
  ///   invoice engine refuses to issue. Both are shown on the charge-review
  ///   screen as something to fix, and NEITHER is guessed at. A guessed rate
  ///   collects money nobody owed; a guessed price bills a number nobody chose.
  unitPrice   Decimal? @map("unit_price") @db.Decimal(14, 2)
  currency    String   @default("INR") @db.Char(3)
  /// The exact-match key into `tax_rules`. NOT the printed HSN.
  taxCategory String?  @map("tax_category") @db.VarChar(64)
  /// The printed HSN/SAC. Presentation only — see `invoice_items`.
  itemCode    String?  @map("item_code") @db.VarChar(32)

  /// ⚠️ WRITTEN BY `services/invoicing`, NEVER BY THIS PROGRAMME. The only link
  ///   back, and the direction of the arrow is the whole seam.
  ///
  /// ⚠️ THE INVOICE AND **NOT** THE INVOICE ITEM, WHICH CORRECTS
  ///   BILLING_INTEGRATION.md's SKETCH. That document says
  ///   "`charge_requests.invoice_item_id` is the only link back", and an item id
  ///   turns out to be unhelpable: `finalizeInvoice` RE-PRICES a draft from its
  ///   stored inputs, and `repriceLoadedDraft` does that by DELETING every
  ///   `invoice_items` row and writing them again. So an item's id changes at
  ///   least once between the draft being raised and the document being issued —
  ///   and any FK onto it either blocks finalisation outright (`Restrict`, which
  ///   is what happened) or silently detaches the link at the moment it starts to
  ///   matter.
  ///
  ///   `invoice_id` is stable for the life of the document, is what every
  ///   question anybody asks is actually about ("which bill did this supply
  ///   reach?"), and — because a charge produces exactly one line and the invoice
  ///   is composed in charge order — the line is recoverable by position where
  ///   anyone ever needs it. Recorded here rather than fixed in the document,
  ///   because the document describes the intent correctly and only the column
  ///   was wrong.
  invoiceId String? @map("invoice_id") @db.Uuid

  /// Why it was suppressed, where it was. Required by CHECK on `SUPPRESSED`:
  /// "we did not bill this" with no reason is indistinguishable from a bug.
  suppressedReason String? @map("suppressed_reason") @db.Text
  /// Who took the decision, where a human did. NULL where the policy decided.
  decidedById      String?   @map("decided_by_id") @db.Uuid
  decidedAt        DateTime? @map("decided_at") @db.Timestamptz(6)

  createdAt DateTime @default(now()) @map("created_at") @db.Timestamptz(6)
  updatedAt DateTime @updatedAt @map("updated_at") @db.Timestamptz(6)

  organization Organization @relation(fields: [organizationId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  branch       Branch       @relation(fields: [organizationId, branchId], references: [organizationId, id], onDelete: Cascade)
  /// Cascade: a charge request has no life apart from the supply it cites, and
  /// nothing may delete a posted dispense line anyway.
  dispenseLine       DispenseLine?       @relation(fields: [organizationId, dispenseLineId], references: [organizationId, id], onDelete: Cascade)
  dispenseReturnLine DispenseReturnLine? @relation(fields: [organizationId, dispenseReturnLineId], references: [organizationId, id], onDelete: Cascade)
  /// Cascade, like the two above: a charge request has no life apart from the
  /// line it cites, and nothing may delete a posted consumption line anyway.
  consumptionLine    ConsumptionLine?    @relation(fields: [organizationId, consumptionLineId], references: [organizationId, id], onDelete: Cascade)
  product            Product             @relation("ChargeRequestProduct", fields: [productId], references: [id], onDelete: Restrict)
  unit               UnitOfMeasure       @relation("ChargeRequestUnit", fields: [unitId], references: [id], onDelete: Restrict)
  /// Restrict, like `dispenses.patient`: a person's billing history is a
  /// retention obligation and outlives their being a current patient.
  patient            Patient?            @relation(fields: [organizationId, patientId], references: [organizationId, id], onDelete: Restrict)
  policyRule         ChargePolicyRule?   @relation(fields: [organizationId, policyRuleId], references: [organizationId, id], onDelete: Restrict, onUpdate: NoAction)
  /// Restrict: an invoice that billed this cannot be hard-deleted out from under
  /// it. An issued invoice is VOIDed, never deleted, and a draft that is
  /// cancelled releases its requests back to `PENDING` explicitly.
  invoice            Invoice?            @relation(fields: [organizationId, invoiceId], references: [organizationId, id], onDelete: Restrict)
  decidedBy          User?               @relation("ChargeRequestDecidedBy", fields: [decidedById], references: [id], onDelete: SetNull)

  @@unique([organizationId, id])
  /// ⚠️ ONE CHARGE REQUEST PER SUPPLIED LINE, AND THIS IS THE IDEMPOTENCY
  ///   GUARANTEE RATHER THAN A TIDINESS ONE. NULLS NOT DISTINCT is deliberately
  ///   NOT applied: an `INVENTORY` request carries no dispense line and a clinic
  ///   has many of those. It is a PARTIAL unique in the migration, `WHERE
  ///   dispense_line_id IS NOT NULL`, which Prisma cannot express — the same
  ///   treatment `invoice_documents_current_per_type_key` gets.
  @@index([organizationId, dispenseLineId])
  @@index([organizationId, dispenseReturnLineId])
  /// The same partial-unique treatment (PI-9): `WHERE consumption_line_id IS NOT
  /// NULL`, in the migration, because Prisma cannot express it and a plain
  /// unique would refuse the many rows that carry none.
  @@index([organizationId, consumptionLineId])
  /// The charge-review screen: what is outstanding at this counter, oldest
  /// first. The one query that renders it.
  @@index([organizationId, branchId, status, occurredAt])
  /// "What does this patient owe for?" — and the reversal's lookup of the
  /// request its original supply raised.
  @@index([organizationId, patientId, occurredAt])
  @@index([organizationId, invoiceId])
  @@map("charge_requests")
}
